My approach was similar but instead of forcing a decision between robbing house i, and not robbing house i (which is where Neetcode takes the max(n + rob1, rob2)) I simply stored the outcome of robbing the ith house at every position in nums.

By appending 0 to the nums array we get: [house1Val, house2Val, house3Val, .... , houseNVal, 0].

And then we can start by considering the subarray [houseN-2Val, houseN-1Val, houseNVal, 0].

If we rob houseN-2Val we simply take the houseN-2Val + max(houseNVal, 0) and overwrite our nums array since this is the max value we can get if we robbed houseN-2, and then we continue in this manner until we get to house1.

At the end we return the max between the first 2 houses, and whichever one yields the max was the optimal starting house.

I only went from the back of the array so that I didn't have to append 0 to the front of the array which would have been a O(N) operation, but the logic works going both ways if you instead append 0 to the front.

class Solution {
public:
    int rob(vector<int>& nums) {

        if (nums.size() == 1)
        {
            return nums[0];
        }

        nums.push_back(0);

        for (int i { static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 4 }; i >= 0; --i)
        {
            nums[i] += max(nums[i + 2], nums[i + 3]);
        }

        return max(nums[0], nums[1]);

    }
};

